# 6. Architecture Principles

The architecture follows these principles:

* Modular Design
* Layered Architecture
* Event-Driven Processing
* Async-First Execution
* Explainable Intelligence
* Vendor Neutrality
* Plugin Extensibility
* Security by Design
* Testability
* Maintainability

---

# 7. Documentation Structure

```text
architecture/
│
├── README.md
├── 01-system-overview.md
├── 02-design-goals.md
├── ...
├── 30-architecture-decisions.md
│
├── diagrams/
│
└── adr/
```

---

# 8. Architecture Governance

Architecture documentation must always remain synchronized with implementation.

Every major architectural change requires:

* Documentation update
* Architecture review
* ADR (Architecture Decision Record)
* Version update (when applicable)

No undocumented architectural change is considered complete.

---

# 9. Diagram Standards

All diagrams must:

* Match the latest implementation.
* Use consistent terminology.
* Reference official glossary terms.
* Avoid implementation-specific details unless required.

Preferred diagram categories include:

* Context
* Layer
* Component
* Data Flow
* Event Flow
* Sequence
* Deployment

---

# 10. Architecture Decision Records (ADR)

Important architectural decisions are documented separately inside the **adr/** directory.

Every ADR should explain:

* Context
* Decision
* Alternatives Considered
* Consequences
* Status

Architecture documents describe the system.

ADRs explain **why** it evolved that way.

---

# 11. Versioning

This documentation follows the project versioning policy.

Major architecture changes require:

* Architecture review
* ADR
* Changelog update
* Documentation update

---

# 12. Contribution Rules

Contributors must:

* Follow official terminology.
* Respect dependency rules.
* Preserve layer boundaries.
* Avoid undocumented architectural shortcuts.
* Keep diagrams synchronized with documentation.

---

# 13. Relationship with Other Documents

This documentation complements:

* identity/architecture.md
* design_rules.md
* coding_standards.md
* constraints.md
* non_functional_requirements.md
* roadmap.md

The Identity package defines **what** the architecture must achieve.

This Architecture Pack explains **how** it achieves those goals.

---

# 14. Future Expansion

The architecture is intentionally designed to support:

* Additional blockchains
* Additional intelligence agents
* Plugin ecosystem
* Distributed processing
* AI model upgrades
* New data providers

without requiring fundamental redesign.

---

# 15. Success Criteria

The Architecture Documentation is considered complete when:

* Every architectural layer is documented.
* Every component has a defined responsibility.
* Every dependency is documented.
* Every major decision has an ADR.
* Documentation remains synchronized with implementation.

---

# 16. Final Statement

The Architecture Documentation is the technical blueprint of the A01 Blockchain Intelligence Agent.

It ensures that every implementation decision is traceable, reviewable, maintainable, and aligned with the long-term vision of the CIE-OS platform.

---

**End of Architecture Documentation Index**
